              "id": "29",
              "name": "Events [Entity Service]"
            },
            "entity": {
              "description": "On Wednesday, Thailand’s constitutional court suspended Prime Minister Prayut Chan-o-cha from official duty pending the result of a legal review of his term limit, according to local media reports. The Thai constitution limits prime ministers to eight years in office. However, the case was brought by opposition parties who argue that Prayut, who came to power in a 2014 coup, has reached his eight-year term limit, AFP reports. According to local media reports, Deputy Prime Minister Prawit Wongsuwan will serve as caretaker prime minister in the meantime.",
              "id": "1562331972956893184",
              "name": "Thai court suspends prime minister from duties pending results of term limit review"
            }
          },
          {
            "domain": {
              "description": "Real world events. ",
              "id": "29",
              "name": "Events [Entity Service]"
            },
            "entity": {
              "description": "Thailand’s constitutional court delivers the outcome of a legal review of Prime Minister Prayut Chan-o-cha's term limit on Friday, after temporarily suspending the PM from official duty in August. The Thai constitution limits prime ministers to eight years in office. However, the case was brought by opposition parties who argue that Prayut, who came to power in a 2014 coup, has reached his eight-year term limit, AFP reports. According to local media reports, police have been deployed across Bangkok in anticipation of potential rallies protesting the result of the ruling.",
              "id": "1575749073302220801",
              "name": "Thai constitutional court to rule on PM's term limit review"
            }
          }
